course: dictionary
lesson: 6498
title: Not necessarily
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Necessarily» is a word for checking whether something is always true. Most often you will see it in the phrase «not necessarily». «A desert is a place with very little rain, not necessarily a hot one.» Some deserts are hot, but not every desert is, so «not necessarily» means not always, not in every case.
explain: The word is a warning. When someone says a thing is true, ask yourself: does it have to be true in every case, or only in some? «A bigger box is not necessarily heavier.» You might find one big box that is light, and that one example shows the rule is not always true.
explain: «Necessarily» can also mean the opposite: it cannot be otherwise, because it must be so. «Two and two necessarily make four.» There is no other possible answer. So look at the sentence around the word to tell which meaning the writer means.
fact: Necessarily means not always, in every case, when it is used in «not necessarily».
fact: «Not necessarily» warns you not to jump to conclusions.
fact: «Popular is not necessarily true.» Many people liking something does not prove it is right.
fact: «Necessarily» can mean it must be so, as in «Two and two necessarily make four.»
fact: One example does not prove a rule. Check more cases before you decide something is always true.
exercise: «A desert is not necessarily a hot place.» What does not necessarily mean here? | Very quickly | Never at all | *Not always, not in every case | Always, every single time
exercise: «Popular is not necessarily true.» What does this sentence warn you to do? | *Not to jump to conclusions | To count how many people agree | To ignore all facts | To believe everything that is popular
exercise: «Two and two necessarily make four.» What does necessarily mean here? | It is probably true | *It cannot be otherwise, because it must be so | It is not always true | It is only a guess
test: Which statement uses «not necessarily» to show something is not always true? | A rule is always proved by one example | *A bigger box is not necessarily heavier | Two and two necessarily make four | The sun is necessarily hot
test: In «Four and four necessarily make eight», what does necessarily mean? | It might be true | It is not always true | *It must be so, it cannot be different | It is a question
exam: Why is «not necessarily» a useful thinking word in science and maths? | It is only used in English lessons | It makes sentences longer | *It stops us believing a rule from one example | It means something is always false
